.TH MEH 1 "2013-12-28"
.SH NAME
meh \- simple, minimalist, super fast image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B meh
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B meh \-list
.RI [ LISTFILE ]
.br
.B meh \-ctl
.br
.B meh \-f
.br
.B meh \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B meh
is a small, simple, super fast image viewer using raw XLib.
It is similar to
.BR feh (1),
but faster and simpler.
.LP
.B meh
can use ImageMagick's
.BR convert (1)
to view almost 200 file formats, though it is slower for these formats.
Built-in formats are JPEG, PNG, BMP, and netpbm.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-list \ [\fILISTFILE\fR]
Treat \fILISTFILE\fR as list of images. Defaults to stdin.
.TP
.B \-ctl
Display files as they are received on stdin.
.TP
.B \-f
Use nearest neighbour interpolation (fast).
.TP
.B \-v
Print version and exit.
.SH DISPLAY DEPTHS
.B meh
renders to whatever visual the X server offers. TrueColor and DirectColor
visuals of any channel layout are supported, including 15 and 16 bit, packed
24 bit, 10 bit deep colour, and MSBFirst servers. Palettised visuals
(PseudoColor, StaticColor, GrayScale, StaticGray) are supported at one byte
per pixel.
.PP
Where several visuals are available at the screen's depth,
.B meh
does not simply take the default one. A TrueColor visual with at least five
bits in every channel is preferred; failing that a writable palette is
preferred over a read-only one, and colour over grey. On a sixteen colour
display this picks a PseudoColor visual with sixteen settable entries over a
TrueColor visual that would have only one or two bits per channel.
.PP
Output coarse enough to band is dithered with an ordered 4x4 pattern.
Formats with five or more bits per channel are never dithered.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MEH_DITHER
Set to
.B 0
to disable dithering on coarse displays. Useful for judging the result by eye
on the hardware, since the trade is banding against noise.
